from <math> import sqrt;

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Coupled inductors — two and three magnetically coupled windings
//
//  SPICE's K element, as a module. Each winding is a branch of the MNA system
//  whose voltage is its resistance drop plus the rate of change of its flux
//  linkage, and the flux linkages are the inductance matrix times the
//  currents:
//
//      v1 = R1 i1 + d/dt (L1 i1 + M i2)          M = k sqrt(L1 L2)
//      v2 = R2 i2 + d/dt (M i1  + L2 i2)
//
//           i1 →                         i2 →
//      p1 ──●─┐                     ┌─●── p2        ● = dotted end
//             )  L1     k     L2   (
//      n1 ────┘                     └──── n2
//
//  Dots are on p1 and p2: a current entering p1 induces a voltage that makes
//  p2 positive with respect to n2. A negative k is the same as moving one dot.
//
//  ── How it is written, and why ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  Each winding is a voltage_source (w1, w2, w3) whose value reads the branch
//  currents of all of them, I(w1), I(w2) ..., and takes ddt() of the flux.
//  The currents are therefore unknowns of the MNA system — the same unknowns
//  an L primitive has — and ddt() is integrated with the active solver's own
//  formula (runtime/vm/state_slots.hpp), so the result is the companion model
//  SPICE builds for coupled inductors, with the inductance matrix L/h in the
//  branch rows. Nothing is integrated by hand and nothing lags a step.
//
//  The flux is written once per winding, as a whole, rather than as an L
//  primitive plus a separate mutual term: one ddt() per winding keeps the
//  stored energy exactly 1/2 i' L i (see Verified), and it lets R1, R2 and the
//  leakage be 0, which an R or L primitive does not.
//
//  ── The coupling coefficient ───────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  |k| <= 1 is required for the windings to be passive (the inductance matrix
//  must be positive semi-definite); nothing checks it, and |k| > 1 is a
//  circuit that creates energy.
//
//  k = 1 works. The inductance matrix is then singular, but the branch rows are
//  not: whatever resistance either winding sees — its own R, a load, a source
//  impedance — makes the system regular, as it does in SPICE. The one circuit
//  that cannot be solved at k = 1 is the one that has no solution: ideal
//  voltage sources imposed on BOTH windings (they would have to be in exactly
//  the turns ratio). Tested at k = 1 with 0 winding resistance, 1 Ohm source
//  impedance and a 100 Ohm load, against ngspice's K = 1: 19.00929 V vs
//  19.00929 V secondary peak.
//
//  ── Using it ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  The windings are galvanically isolated, so a side that connects to nothing
//  but its own load has no DC reference: its common-mode voltage is
//  undetermined and the matrix is singular in exact arithmetic (SPICE reports
//  "singular matrix" there). In testing, a floating 100 Ohm secondary came out
//  at 0 V common mode with the correct differential voltage, but that is what
//  the LU happened to do with a zero pivot, not something the solver
//  promises. Ground one end of every isolated side, or tie it to ground
//  through a large resistor.
//
//  Branch currents are I(<instance>.w1), I(<instance>.w2), ... — positive into
//  the p terminal — and are .save-able like any branch.
//
//  At DC, and in .op, the windings are what inductors are: shorts (their R
//  only). With `.op;` an AC source whose value at t = 0 is not zero therefore
//  starts with that value treated as DC, i.e. a large current through the
//  winding resistance that then decays with L/R — the same as SPICE. Start
//  such sources at a zero crossing or leave .op out.
//
//  Use ndf2 or bdf2. trapezoidal matches them on smooth sine-driven decks but
//  drifts once steps converge in one Newton iteration (see <passives>' header
//  for the measurement); that is a runtime limitation of trapezoidal with any
//  ddt()-driven source, not of this model.
//
//  ── Verified ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  CoupledInductors 1 mH / 4 mH, k = 0.9, 1 Ohm source, 100 Ohm load, 10 V
//  1 kHz, against the closed-form phasor solution and an ngspice twin
//  (L, L, K): secondary 17.20910 V / +6.1139 deg (analytic 17.20924 V /
//  +6.1141 deg, ngspice 17.20909 V); whole waveform within 1.4e-4 of ngspice
//  on ndf2, bdf2 and trapezoidal, with and without .op. Energy: the
//  integrated terminal power equals 1/2 i' L i to 7.5e-8 J out of a
//  3.1e-3 J peak (k = 0.9 and k = 1). .ac impedance with k = 0.95 and
//  R1 = 0.5, R2 = 1 Ohm, 10 Hz - 100 MHz: 4.4e-10 relative.
//
//  CoupledInductors3 1 / 4 / 0.25 mH, k = 0.95 / 0.9 / 0.85, all three
//  loaded, against ngspice with three K elements: 17.26305 V vs 17.26304 V
//  and 4.050154 V vs 4.050154 V, phases within 2e-4 deg, winding currents
//  within 2e-5 over the run (examples/Magnetics/coupled_inductors3.hvr).
//
//  Not modelled: saturation (<magnetics> SaturableInductor /
//  SaturableTransformer), core loss, winding capacitance, frequency-dependent
//  winding resistance.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Two coupled windings.
//
//   L1, L2   self inductances, H (> 0)
//   k        coupling coefficient, -1 <= k <= 1
//   R1, R2   winding resistances, Ohm                      0
analog module CoupledInductors<double L1, double L2, double k,
                               double R1 = 0, double R2 = 0>()
    [wire p1, wire n1, wire p2, wire n2]
{
    double M  = k * sqrt(L1 * L2);
    double i1 = I(w1);
    double i2 = I(w2);
    double v1 = R1 * i1 + ddt(L1 * i1 + M * i2);
    double v2 = R2 * i2 + ddt(M * i1 + L2 * i2);
    voltage_source w1<>(v1) [p1, n1];
    voltage_source w2<>(v2) [p2, n2];
}

// Three coupled windings, one coupling coefficient per pair.
//
//   L1, L2, L3       self inductances, H (> 0)
//   k12, k13, k23    pairwise coupling coefficients
//   R1, R2, R3       winding resistances, Ohm              0
//
// Each |k| <= 1 is not sufficient on its own: the 3x3 inductance matrix must
// be positive semi-definite, i.e. also
// 1 - k12^2 - k13^2 - k23^2 + 2 k12 k13 k23 >= 0. A three-winding transformer
// with all k = 1 satisfies it with equality.
analog module CoupledInductors3<double L1, double L2, double L3,
                                double k12, double k13, double k23,
                                double R1 = 0, double R2 = 0, double R3 = 0>()
    [wire p1, wire n1, wire p2, wire n2, wire p3, wire n3]
{
    double M12 = k12 * sqrt(L1 * L2);
    double M13 = k13 * sqrt(L1 * L3);
    double M23 = k23 * sqrt(L2 * L3);
    double i1 = I(w1);
    double i2 = I(w2);
    double i3 = I(w3);
    double v1 = R1 * i1 + ddt(L1  * i1 + M12 * i2 + M13 * i3);
    double v2 = R2 * i2 + ddt(M12 * i1 + L2  * i2 + M23 * i3);
    double v3 = R3 * i3 + ddt(M13 * i1 + M23 * i2 + L3  * i3);
    voltage_source w1<>(v1) [p1, n1];
    voltage_source w2<>(v2) [p2, n2];
    voltage_source w3<>(v3) [p3, n3];
}
